Q: What is OIDC-style auth? How is it different from “homemade JWT login”?

OIDC = **OpenID Connect**.
Identity layer **on top of OAuth 2.0**.
Used so apps don’t store/check passwords themselves — an **Identity Provider (IdP)** does (Keycloak, Auth0, Okta, Azure AD, Google…).

You already know JWT. OIDC commonly **delivers identity as JWTs** (ID token / access token), issued and signed by the IdP.

────────────────────────────────────────
One-liner

“OAuth 2.0 authorizes access to APIs; OpenID Connect adds login/identity (who the user is), usually with an ID token JWT from an IdP like Keycloak.”

────────────────────────────────────────
❓ Are OAuth 2.0 and OIDC the same?

NO — related, not the same.

| | OAuth 2.0 | OpenID Connect (OIDC) |
| - | --------- | --------------------- |
| What | Authorization framework | Authentication / identity layer |
| Question it answers | “What is this client allowed to access?” | “Who is the user?” |
| Main token | Access token (optional refresh) | ID token (+ usually access token) |
| Built on | — | Built **on top of** OAuth 2.0 |
| Alone enough for login? | Not really (no standard user identity) | Yes — designed for login/SSO |

Simple analogy:
- OAuth 2.0 = “valet key to use a service” (permission to call APIs)
- OIDC = “ID card that says who you are” (login), using OAuth machinery

People say “OAuth login” casually, but real standardized login is usually **OIDC** (which uses OAuth 2.0 under the hood).

Remember:
OIDC = OAuth 2.0 + identity (ID Token + UserInfo)

────────────────────────────────────────
Core roles

| Role | Meaning |
| ---- | ------- |
| User (Resource Owner) | Person logging in |
| Client (App) | Your SPA / mobile / backend |
| IdP / Authorization Server | Keycloak etc. — authenticates user, issues tokens |
| Resource Server (API) | Your Spring/Quarkus API — validates access token |

────────────────────────────────────────
Typical browser / SPA flow (Authorization Code + PKCE)

1. User opens your app
2. App redirects to IdP login (Keycloak)
3. User enters credentials **at IdP** (not at your API)
4. IdP redirects back with **authorization code**
5. App (or BFF) exchanges code for tokens:
   - **ID Token** → who is the user (OIDC)
   - **Access Token** → call APIs (OAuth)
   - optional **Refresh Token**
6. App calls your API:
   `Authorization: Bearer <access_token>`
7. API validates token (signature via IdP JWKS, exp, issuer, audience) → allow/deny

────────────────────────────────────────
OIDC tokens (interview must-know)

1) ID Token
- Proves authentication
- JWT with claims: `sub`, `email`, `name`, `iss`, `aud`, `exp`
- For the **client app** to know who logged in

2) Access Token
- Used to access **APIs**
- Often JWT (can be opaque in some setups)
- API checks this, not the password

3) Refresh Token
- Get new access tokens without re-login
- Store carefully; rotate/revoke on logout

────────────────────────────────────────
Your “custom JWT login” vs OIDC

| Homemade JWT (what you built) | OIDC style |
| ----------------------------- | ---------- |
| `/login` checks DB password | IdP checks credentials |
| Your app signs JWT with your secret | IdP signs tokens (keys from JWKS) |
| You manage users/password reset/MFA | IdP manages identity features |
| Simple for one app | Standard for SSO across many apps |
| Good learning / internal APIs | Enterprise / multi-app / compliance |

Both can use Bearer JWT on APIs.
Difference is **who issues identity** and **protocol**.

────────────────────────────────────────
Spring Boot (high level)

Common stack:
- `spring-boot-starter-oauth2-resource-server` → API validates JWT
- `spring-boot-starter-oauth2-client` → app logs in via IdP

Resource server idea:
spring.security.oauth2.resourceserver.jwt.issuer-uri=https://keycloak.../realms/myrealm

Spring fetches IdP metadata/JWKS and validates Bearer tokens automatically
(vs manual JwtFilter + shared secret in simple demos).

────────────────────────────────────────
Quarkus (high level)

quarkus-oidc / smallrye-jwt extensions
Configure IdP URL; protect JAX-RS with roles.
Same idea: validate bearer token from Keycloak.

See: `Quarkus_Interview_Questions/Quarkus Security JWT and OIDC overview`

────────────────────────────────────────
Why companies use OIDC/Keycloak

- Single Sign-On (SSO) across apps
- Central user management
- MFA, social login, password policies
- Standard protocols (not custom auth every service)
- Easier compliance / audit

────────────────────────────────────────
Security reminders

- Prefer Authorization Code + PKCE for SPAs (not implicit flow)
- Validate `iss`, `aud`, `exp`, signature
- Access token in Authorization header (CSRF usually not an issue)
- If token in cookie → CSRF concerns return
- Don’t put secrets in frontend; use PKCE / BFF patterns as needed

────────────────────────────────────────
Interview answers

What is OIDC?
“OpenID Connect is an identity layer on OAuth 2.0. Users authenticate at an IdP like Keycloak; the app receives ID/access tokens and APIs trust the access token as a Bearer JWT.”

Difference from your Spring JWT filter project?
“In my JWT demo, my service issued tokens after local password check. In OIDC, Keycloak issues tokens after login; my API is mainly a resource server that validates those tokens.”

OAuth vs OIDC?
“OAuth 2.0 = delegated authorization (access APIs). OIDC = authentication/identity (who you are), built on OAuth.”

